  "reasoning": "Step 1: Analyze the patient's symptoms. The patient reports migraines, regular headaches, distorted eyesight, excessive appetite, a painful neck, melancholy, irritability, acid reflux, and indigestion.\nStep 2: Evaluate candidate diagnoses. Gastroesophageal reflux disease and peptic ulcer disease could explain the acid reflux and indigestion, but they do not account for the migraines, distorted vision, neck pain, or mood disturbances. Cervical spondylosis explains the neck pain but fails to cover the neurological, visual, and gastrointestinal symptoms.\nStep 3: Compare with training references. Reference [1] presents an nearly identical symptom profile (headaches, stomach acid, indigestion, hazy/distorted vision, increased appetite, stiff neck, depression, irritation) and is diagnosed as migraine. Clinically, visual auras (distorted eyesight), mood changes (melancholy, irritability), neck stiffness, and autonomic/GI symptoms (acid reflux, indigestion) are classic prodromal, ictal, or postdromal features of migraines.\nStep 4: Select the best fit. Migraine is the single unit that comprehensively covers all the distinct symptoms presented in the case.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]migraine[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
